      "text": "To find the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. This means we need an integer \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nWe can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start with the Euclidean algorithm to find the gcd of 11 and 100:\n\\[ 100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1, \\]\n\\[ 11 = 11 \\cdot 1 + 0. \\]\nThe gcd is 1, and we can express 1 as a combination of 11 and 100:\n\\[ 1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11. \\]\nThis tells us that:\n\\[ -9 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}, \\]\nso the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\( -9 \\). Since we want a positive inverse, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[ -9 + 100 = 91. \\]\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by 91:\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nWe calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[ 36 \\cdot 91 = 3276. \\]\nNext, we find the last two digits of 3276 by taking 3276 modulo 100:\n\\[ 3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}. \\]\nTherefore, the value of \\( t \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{76}. \\]",
