      "text": "To find a two-digit integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[ 11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nFirst, we need the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. This is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\\[ 11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nWe can find \\( x \\) using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. Applying the Euclidean Algorithm to 100 and 11, we get:\n\\[ 100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1, \\]\n\\[ 1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11. \\]\n\nFrom this, we see that:\n\\[ -9 \\cdot 11 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}, \\]\nso the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\( -9 \\). Since we want a positive integer, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\\[ -9 \\equiv 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nThus, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by 91:\n\\[ t \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nWe calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\\[ 36 \\cdot 91 = 3276. \\]\n\nNext, we find the last two digits of 3276 by taking \\( 3276 \\mod 100 \\):\n\\[ 3276 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100}. \\]\n\nTherefore, \\( t \\) must be 76. We can verify this solution by checking that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot 76 \\) are indeed 36:\n\\[ 11 \\cdot 76 = 836, \\]\nand the last two digits of 836 are 36, confirming our solution.\n\nThe value of \\( t \\) is \\(\\boxed{76}\\).\n\nJames buys 3 shirts for $60. There is a 40% off sale. How much did he pay per shirt after the discount?\n\nTo determine how much James paid per shirt after the discount, let's follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the original price per shirt:**\n   James buys 3 shirts for $60. Therefore, the original price per shirt is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Original price per shirt} = \\frac{60}{3} = 20 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the discount amount for each shirt:**\n   The discount is 40%. Therefore, the discount per shirt is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount per shirt} = 20 \\times 0.40 = 8 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the price per shirt after the discount:**\n   The price per shirt after the discount is the original price per shirt minus the discount per shirt:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Price per shirt after discount} = 20 - 8 = 12 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the amount James paid per shirt after the discount is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).\n\nAfter applying the discount, James now buys 3 shirts for $60 at a 40% discount.\n\nA regular decagon is inscribed in a circle of radius 6. Find the number of degrees in the measure of the contained angle.\n\nTo determine the number of degrees in the measure of the central angle of a regular decagon inscribed in a circle of radius 6, we start by recalling the formula for the measure of a central angle of a regular polygon. The measure of a central angle of a regular \\(n\\)-sided polygon inscribed in a circle of radius \\(r\\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Central angle} = \\frac{360^\\circ}{n}\n\\]\n\nFor a decagon, \\(n = 10\\). Therefore, the measure of the central angle is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Central angle} = \\frac{360^\\circ}{10} = 36^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of degrees in the measure of the central angle is \\(\\boxed{36}\\).",
