      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) such that the last two digits of \\( 11 \\cdot t \\) are 36, we need to find \\( t \\) such that:\n\n\\[\n11t \\equiv 36 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100. This is a number \\( x \\) such that:\n\n\\[\n11x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nWe use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find this inverse. We start by applying the Euclidean Algorithm to 100 and 11:\n\n\\[\n100 = 9 \\cdot 11 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n1 = 100 - 9 \\cdot 11\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can see that:\n\n\\[\n1 \\equiv -9 \\cdot 11 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nThus, the multiplicative inverse of 11 modulo 100 is \\(-9\\). Since we need a positive integer, we convert \\(-9\\) to a positive equivalent by adding 100:\n\n\\[\n-9 + 100 = 91\n\\]\n\nSo, the inverse of 11 modulo 100 is 91. Now, we can solve the original congruence by multiplying both sides by 91:\n\n\\[\nt \\equiv 36 \\cdot 91 \\pmod{100}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\( 36 \\cdot 91 \\):\n\n\\[\n36 \\cdot 91 = 3276\n\\]\n\nWe need the last two digits of 3276, which is:\n\n\\[\n3276 \\mod 100 = 76\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\( t \\equiv 76 \\pmod{100} \\). Since \\( t \\) is a two-digit positive integer, the solution is:\n\n\\[\nt = 76\n\\]\n\nThus, the unique two-digit positive integer \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
